# Layouts vs Pages (layout.js / page.js) ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

## 🤯 Problem

Every page in your School ERP

needs the SAME Navbar and Sidebar.

Do you import and repeat

`<Navbar /><Sidebar />`

at the top of EVERY single page component?

That's tedious and duplicated everywhere.

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Imagine a School Building

with a Reception Desk and Hallway

that stays THE SAME

no matter which classroom (page) you walk into.

You don't rebuild the reception desk

for every single classroom —

it just WRAPS around whichever room you enter.

`layout.js` is that shared building shell.

---

## ✅ What is it?

`page.js` → the UNIQUE content for a specific route.

`layout.js` → shared UI that WRAPS around a page

(and all its nested pages), like Navbars, Sidebars, Footers.

Layouts persist across navigation — they don't re-render/remount.

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

To avoid repeating shared UI (Navbar, Sidebar) on every page.

Layouts PRESERVE their state across route changes

(e.g. a sidebar's scroll position doesn't reset when you switch pages).

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

🏫 The Reception Desk That Never Changes

Layout = the building shell (Navbar, Sidebar) — stays put.

Page = the specific classroom's content — changes per route.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

```
app/
  layout.js       → Navbar + Sidebar (shared across ALL pages)
  students/page.js → just the student list content
  teachers/page.js  → just the teacher list content
```

Switching between Students and Teachers pages

keeps the Navbar/Sidebar mounted and untouched —

only the inner page content swaps.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Thinking every folder NEEDS its own `layout.js`.

Layouts are OPTIONAL per folder —

nested folders automatically inherit the PARENT layout

unless they define their own.

---

## 💻 Pattern

```jsx
// app/layout.js
export default function RootLayout({ children }) {
    return (
        <html>
            <body>
                <Navbar />
                <main>{children}</main>
            </body>
        </html>
    );
}
```

```jsx
// app/students/page.js
export default function StudentsPage() {
    return <h1>Students List</h1>; // renders inside {children} above
}
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

Exactly like Angular's `app.component.html`

with a `<router-outlet>` inside it —

the outer shell (Navbar, Sidebar) stays fixed,

and the routed content swaps inside the outlet.

`layout.js` = the shell, `page.js` content = what fills `<router-outlet>`.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

In the Next.js App Router, page.js defines the unique content for a specific route, while layout.js defines shared UI that wraps around a page and its nested routes, such as a Navbar or Sidebar. Layouts persist across navigation without re-rendering, and nested routes automatically inherit their parent layout unless they define their own.

---

