# Dynamic Routes ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

## 🤯 Problem

You need a page for EVERY individual student —

`/students/1`, `/students/2`, `/students/3`...

You obviously can't create a separate folder

for every possible student ID by hand.

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Imagine a Filing Cabinet drawer

labeled "Student File #___" —

ONE drawer template,

that works for ANY student ID you slide in,

instead of a separate physical drawer for every single student.

---

## ✅ What is it?

A folder named with SQUARE BRACKETS,

like `[id]`,

creates a DYNAMIC route

that matches ANY value in that URL position.

The matched value is available as a route parameter.

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

To create ONE template page

that works for MANY items sharing the same structure

(one student profile page, reused for every student ID).

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

🗂 The Reusable Drawer Template

`[id]` = "any value goes here" —

ONE page file, works for EVERY matching ID.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

```
app/students/[id]/page.js
```

Visiting `/students/45` or `/students/102`

BOTH use this SAME file,

with `id` available as `45` or `102` respectively

inside the component.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Confusing `[id]` (single dynamic segment)

with `[...slug]` (catch-all, matches MULTIPLE segments).

`[id]` matches exactly ONE URL segment.

`[...slug]` matches an ENTIRE remaining path, as an array.

---

## 💻 Pattern

```jsx
// app/students/[id]/page.js
export default function StudentPage({ params }) {
    return <h1>Student ID: {params.id}</h1>;
}
```

```
/students/45  →  params.id === "45"
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

Exactly like Angular's route param syntax:

`{ path: 'students/:id', component: StudentComponent }`

read via `ActivatedRoute.snapshot.paramMap.get('id')` —

Next.js just uses `[id]` folder naming instead of a config object.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

Dynamic Routes in Next.js are created using square-bracket folder names, like [id], which match any value at that position in the URL. The matched value is passed into the page component through the params prop, allowing a single page file to serve many URLs that share the same structure, such as individual student profile pages.

---

## 📝 Keywords

Dynamic Route

[id]

params

Catch-all Route

