# API Routes (Route Handlers) ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

## 🤯 Problem

Your Next.js frontend needs a small backend endpoint

(e.g. handle a contact form submission).

Do you REALLY need to spin up

a SEPARATE Express server

just for a few simple API endpoints?

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Imagine the School Office

having BOTH the Reception Desk (frontend pages)

AND a small back-office counter (API endpoint)

in the SAME building —

no need to build a whole separate office across town.

---

## ✅ What is it?

A `route.js` file inside the `app/` directory

defines a BACKEND API endpoint,

directly inside your Next.js project —

handling `GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE` like a mini Express route.

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

For small-to-medium backend logic

(contact forms, simple CRUD, webhook handlers)

WITHOUT needing a fully separate Express server.

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

🏢 One Building, Two Counters

Reception (pages) and Back Office (API routes)

live in the SAME Next.js project,

no separate server needed.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

```
app/api/contact/route.js
```

Handles `POST /api/contact`

when a visitor submits the school's contact form —

all inside the SAME Next.js app that renders the pages.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Naming the file anything other than `route.js`

inside an API folder.

Just like `page.js` is required for pages,

`route.js` is the REQUIRED filename

for a folder to become an API endpoint.

---

## 💻 Pattern

```js
// app/api/contact/route.js
export async function POST(request) {
    const data = await request.json();
    console.log(data); // { name, email, message }
    return Response.json({ success: true });
}

export async function GET() {
    return Response.json({ message: "Contact API is live" });
}
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

There's no Angular equivalent —

Angular is purely a frontend framework.

This is closer to writing an Express route handler,

just co-located inside the Next.js project itself.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

Route Handlers, defined in a route.js file inside the app directory, let you build backend API endpoints directly within a Next.js project. They export functions named after HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) and handle requests similarly to an Express route, making it possible to build small-to-medium backend logic without a separate server.

---

