# Data Fetching & Caching ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

## 🤯 Problem

You call `fetch()` inside a Server Component.

Sometimes you want ALWAYS-fresh data (attendance right now).

Sometimes you want it cached for speed (school's About page).

Sometimes you want it refreshed every hour (class summary).

How does ONE `fetch()` call support all three behaviors?

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Imagine ordering a Newspaper subscription

with different delivery rules:

"Bring me a BRAND NEW paper every single morning" (no cache).

"Just give me the SAME paper you printed once, forever" (static cache).

"Reprint it once every few hours, but keep serving the last copy meanwhile" (revalidate).

Next.js's extended `fetch()` lets you choose the rule per request.

---

## ✅ What is it?

Next.js extends the native `fetch()` API

with caching options:

`cache: 'force-cache'` (default) → cache indefinitely, like SSG.

`cache: 'no-store'` → never cache, fresh every request, like SSR.

`next: { revalidate: N }` → cache, but refresh every N seconds, like ISR.

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

To control EXACTLY how fresh vs fast

each piece of data on a page should be,

per individual fetch call, without extra configuration files.

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

📰 Three Newspaper Delivery Rules

`force-cache` = printed once, same copy forever.

`no-store` = brand new copy every single time.

`revalidate: N` = reprinted every N seconds.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

```js
fetch('/api/about');                                  // cached forever (SSG-like)
fetch('/api/attendance-today', { cache: 'no-store' }); // always fresh (SSR-like)
fetch('/api/class-summary', { next: { revalidate: 3600 } }); // refreshed hourly (ISR-like)
```

All three fetches can live on DIFFERENT pages

with completely different freshness needs.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Assuming ALL `fetch()` calls in Next.js

behave like a normal browser `fetch()`.

Next.js's `fetch()` is EXTENDED with its OWN caching layer —

the default behavior (`force-cache`) is different from a plain browser fetch,

which is a common source of "why isn't my data updating?" bugs.

---

## 💻 Pattern

```jsx
async function Page() {
    const fresh = await fetch(url, { cache: 'no-store' });
    const cached = await fetch(url); // default: force-cache
    const revalidated = await fetch(url, { next: { revalidate: 60 } });
}
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

Similar to configuring an HTTP Interceptor

or RxJS `shareReplay` with a specific caching strategy —

except Next.js bakes these caching STRATEGIES

directly into the `fetch()` call itself.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

Next.js extends the native fetch API with built-in caching controls: cache: 'force-cache' (the default) caches data indefinitely, similar to static generation; cache: 'no-store' disables caching entirely for always-fresh data, similar to server-side rendering; and next: { revalidate: N } caches data but automatically refreshes it after N seconds, similar to incremental static regeneration. This lets each fetch call on a page choose its own freshness strategy.

---

