# Middleware ⭐⭐⭐⭐☆

## 🤯 Problem

An unauthenticated user directly types

`/dashboard`

into the browser address bar.

You want to REDIRECT them to `/login`

BEFORE the dashboard page even starts rendering —

not after it briefly flashes on screen.

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Imagine a Security Checkpoint

BEFORE the main school gate,

checking every visitor's ID

BEFORE they even reach the building entrance —

not after they've already walked halfway down the hallway.

Next.js Middleware runs at that exact checkpoint,

before any page renders.

---

## ✅ What is it?

A `middleware.js` file at the project root

runs CODE BEFORE a request completes,

for matching routes — able to redirect, rewrite,

or modify the request/response.

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

Authentication checks BEFORE a protected page renders.

Redirects based on cookies, geolocation, or A/B testing.

Runs at the EDGE, very close to the user, very fast.

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

🛂 The Checkpoint Before the Gate

Middleware runs BEFORE the page,

not after — stopping unauthorized access at the door,

not after letting someone halfway inside.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

```js
// middleware.js
export function middleware(request) {
    const token = request.cookies.get('token');
    if (!token && request.nextUrl.pathname.startsWith('/dashboard')) {
        return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/login', request.url));
    }
}
```

Any unauthenticated visit to `/dashboard`

is redirected to `/login`

BEFORE the dashboard ever starts rendering.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Putting HEAVY logic (like full database queries) inside Middleware.

Middleware runs on the EDGE runtime,

which has LIMITED capabilities compared to a normal Node.js server —

keep it lightweight (auth checks, redirects), not full business logic.

---

## 💻 Pattern

```js
// middleware.js
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server';

export function middleware(request) {
    // runs before matching requests complete
    return NextResponse.next();
}

export const config = {
    matcher: '/dashboard/:path*',
};
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

Conceptually similar to an Angular Route Guard

(`CanActivate`) —

both intercept navigation

and can block/redirect BEFORE the target page loads.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

Next.js Middleware runs code before a request completes, for routes matching a configured pattern. It's commonly used for authentication checks, redirects, or request rewriting, running at the edge for very low latency. It's conceptually similar to an Angular route guard — both intercept navigation and can redirect before the target page ever renders.

---

## 📝 Keywords

middleware.js

Edge Runtime

NextResponse

Route Guard

Authentication Check

