# loading.js & error.js ⭐⭐⭐⭐☆

## 🤯 Problem

The Students page fetches data from a slow API.

While it's loading, the user sees NOTHING — a blank white screen.

If the fetch FAILS entirely,

the whole app can crash with an unhandled error.

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Imagine walking into an Office

and being told "Please Wait" (a loading sign)

while the clerk finds your file,

instead of standing in dead silence, unsure if anyone even noticed you.

And if something goes wrong,

a clear "Something Went Wrong, Try Again" sign (an error page)

instead of the whole building going dark.

---

## ✅ What is it?

`loading.js` in a route folder →

automatically shown WHILE that page's data is loading.

`error.js` in a route folder →

automatically shown IF that page throws an error,

WITHOUT crashing the entire app.

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

Better user experience — instant loading feedback, no blank screens.

Errors are CONTAINED to just that section of the app,

instead of crashing everything.

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

⏳ Please Wait / ⚠ Something Went Wrong

`loading.js` = the "please wait" sign.

`error.js` = the "something went wrong, retry" sign —

both automatic, both scoped to just that route.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

```
app/students/
  page.js     → the actual student list
  loading.js  → shown instantly while data is fetching
  error.js    → shown if the fetch fails, with a "Try Again" button
```

Users NEVER see a blank screen or a fully crashed app —

just a smooth loading state, or a graceful error message.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Thinking `error.js` catches EVERY error in the whole app.

`error.js` only catches errors

within its OWN route segment (and nested children) —

it's effectively a React Error Boundary, scoped per route.

---

## 💻 Pattern

```jsx
// app/students/loading.js
export default function Loading() {
    return <p>Loading students...</p>;
}
```

```jsx
// app/students/error.js
"use client";
export default function Error({ error, reset }) {
    return (
        <div>
            <p>Something went wrong: {error.message}</p>
            <button onClick={() => reset()}>Try Again</button>
        </div>
    );
}
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

`loading.js` = similar to showing a spinner

while an Angular Resolver loads route data.

`error.js` = conceptually similar to a React Error Boundary,

scoped automatically per route by Next.js.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

loading.js is a special file that Next.js automatically shows while a route segment's data is being fetched, using React Suspense under the hood. error.js is automatically shown if that route segment throws an error, acting like a scoped Error Boundary, containing the failure to just that section instead of crashing the entire application.

